WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.169
Welcome back to the Cyber Security Architecture Series.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:08.708
In previous videos, we covered security principles,
some of the foundational concepts,

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:12.212
and then we started talking
about different cybersecurity domains like identity

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:15.582
and access management
and endpoint security and network security.

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.585
Today, we're going to talk about application security.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:20.320
So let's get started.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.622
What are some of the things
that we need to consider in this?

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:25.525
Why do we have to care, maybe, is the best question.

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:30.163
Well, it turns out that essentially all software has bugs.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.567
Nobody writes a software of any real complexity
that's error free.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:38.104
And it turns out that some percentage of those

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.840
bugs will be security vulnerabilities.

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:44.911
So therefore, if you follow the logical conclusion,

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:49.015
that means essentially all software
is going to have security vulnerabilities in it.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:54.320
So what can we do to try to reduce those
and why do we need to do it?

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:57.924
Obviously, we don't want buggy software
and we don't want security issues,

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:00.960
but this will help drive the point home a little bit,
I think.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.731
If we look at the various stages of application

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:08.635
development and look at where security

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:12.205
or vulnerabilities in general are introduced.

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:14.507
Think about the injection phase.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.844
It turns out that most of the vulnerabilities

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:21.614
and bugs are introduced in the coding phase,
which is not surprising.

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:24.651
And then as we move to unit test,
functional test, system test

00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:27.520
and release, then we find fewer and fewer bugs.

00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:32.192
So that's the green curve,
but that's when they're introduced.

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.259
When are they found?

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.428
Well, they're found as we kind of move along

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:39.232
this process. During the coding phase is
when we're introducing them, but not finding them;

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.401
in the testing phase, we're finding them.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:44.671
And then hopefully when we get to the real world,
we don't find quite so many.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:48.441
Now the interesting thing about all of this is the cost.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.110
How does the cost go?

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:54.247
Well, it turns out cost goes about like this, where we go from 1x

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:59.119
as the whatever you want to base the cost
to fix a bug down here in the coding phase

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:02.922
to -- in some cases 640x.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:07.427
It is vastly more expensive to fix a vulnerablity

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:10.263
once it's in the field than it is to catch it early.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.800
So there's a huge incentive for us to get this right
and get it early.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.971
Now, what can we do then
in order to get this done more quickly?

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:20.373
Well, let's take a look.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.010
We're going to take a look, by the way,
at this software development lifecycle.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.445
That's the SDLC.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.480
And traditionally, this is how it's been done.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.050
We have some design phase here
where we're going to figure out

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:34.220
in general what we're going to do in this particular application.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:37.023
And then we're going to move to a coding phase.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.792
We're going to write the application itself.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.428
Then we're going to go to some sort of testing phase.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:46.132
And then ultimately we release it to the world
and put it in production.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:51.871
Now, what is traditionally happened
is there's a big line separating these two--where

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:56.509
this is the dev part of the process
and this is the ops part of the process.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.411
This is where we're developing.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:03.082
This is where we're releasing and then just operating it
and running it in a continuous state.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:07.654
The problem with this traditional approach
is that it's very linear.

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:10.056
You can see how this process goes.

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.891
It's also very siloed.

00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:16.563
This line right
here could be very thick in some organizations.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:20.633
And it kind of leads to this sort of “over the wall” mentality.

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:23.970
I'm going to write the code and then I'm going
to throw it over there and make them operate it.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.506
And so there's not a lot of communication happening here.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:30.043
It can be a slow process and it's fairly inflexible.

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:35.615
And by the way, I'm going to suggest to you, we didn't
really introduce security very early in this process.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:40.153
Very often security gets introduced out here,
and that's a problem.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:44.824
Now, a more modern approach to this
is this thing called DevOps.

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:50.396
So here we take the two processes, dev,
where we're building the code,

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:54.634
then we're releasing it, we're deploying it,
we're operating it, and we're feeding back.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:04:00.473
With a DevOps process,
now what we've got is a cyclical type of situation.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:03.176
This thing doubles back on itself.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:06.479
There's a feedback loop of continuous improvement.

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:09.349
There's no over the wall. There's no us versus them.

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.751
It's an integrated process.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:14.887
It's much more rapid, and it's designed for agility.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:20.627
So this is a much more flexible kind of capability
moving away from this traditional linear approach.

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:24.764
But again, we haven't really addressed the security here.

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:28.334
And so what more modern approaches have done

00:04:28.635 --> 00:04:34.140
is introduce this notion of devSecOps,
where now we're going to basically bathe

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.910
or encompass the whole thing in a security layer.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:41.281
We're going to put security at every one of these phases.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.951
We don't want this to be something that we wait to the end.

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:47.620
Security can't be a bolt-on.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.289
That is not going to be effective.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:53.826
We need security to be built in
and looked at at every one of these phases.

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:59.432
So, for instance, with a devSecOps approach, we're
going to do what is referred to as shift left thinking.

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:05.805
That if you were thinking of this in a linear phase
or thinking of it this way, then we're going to put security

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:09.142
not just here, we're going to introduce security
at each one of these phases.

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:12.345
We're going to do security by design.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:15.515
We're going to design the system so that it stands up

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:18.418
and it's resilient to attack from the first.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:20.753
Not again as a bolt-on.

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:25.124
Then we're going to create collaboration
among what have been traditionally three

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:27.994
different groups that maybe didn't always talk together.

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:33.933
But with devSecOps, we have it all working together
with a lot of collaboration, a lot of feedback.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.703
And then ultimately try to leverage a lot of automation.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:40.506
I'll talk about that
in terms of tooling a little bit later in the video.

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:42.041
Okay.

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:44.777
Now, we've covered the software development lifecycle.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:48.915
Next, we're going to take a look at secure coding practices
and things like that.

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:51.451
What do we need in order to write secure code?

00:05:51.451 --> 00:05:55.021
If we're going to shift
security left all the way to the coding,

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:58.591
maybe even design phase,
what we're going to focus on the coding part right here.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:00.026
What are my needs?

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:04.063
Well one thing I need is a list of secure coding practices.

00:06:04.464 --> 00:06:09.602
This is a prescriptive way of saying
this is how we should go about writing certain code.

00:06:09.936 --> 00:06:12.004
There are certain things that we need to do.

00:06:12.138 --> 00:06:14.307
For instance, validate inputs.

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:19.946
We need to make sure that if a buffer
has been allocated for this size, for input,

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:26.085
that when someone sends me something, it's not this big
because then in that case, it could be overwriting memory.

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:27.854
We call this a buffer overflow.

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:30.523
So that's a type of condition
that's been around for a long time.

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:33.426
If we don't do all the input checking,
we could have problems.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:37.029
We need to specify how we would do authentication.

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.932
What are some of the other ways
that we would use cryptography?

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.201
What kinds of error handling routines?

00:06:42.201 --> 00:06:46.038
So it's basically a way of saying,
have we checked off all of these things?

00:06:46.038 --> 00:06:47.707
Have we done them all correctly?

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:50.042
Well, there's a great resource here.

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:53.413
It's called owasp.org.

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:55.181
And at this

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:58.951
website, what you'll find
is they have a list of secure coding practices.

00:06:58.951 --> 00:07:03.389
They have a very good document that is kind of an industry
standard that a lot of people use in this.

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:06.392
So I would reference that you take a look there.

00:07:06.993 --> 00:07:08.661
Trusted libraries.

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:13.332
Everyone doesn't write all their code from scratch anymore
because there's just too much that needs to be done.

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:17.236
So as a result, we end up relying on code
that's been written by other people.

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:21.941
It might be open source,
it might be proprietary, but we're going to use this

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:25.111
in our code as well, or make calls to it and leverage it.

00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:29.515
There's a lot of sources that are out there,
but not all of them are trustworthy.

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:32.385
And even the trustworthy ones need to be

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:36.856
looked at with a certain amount of skepticism
and a certain amount of inspection.

00:07:37.156 --> 00:07:40.460
So trusted libraries are an important thing here as well.

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:44.130
And if you want to see what can happen,
even with a trusted library,

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:47.266
go look up something called Log4J.

00:07:48.201 --> 00:07:52.738
This is a very well known vulnerability
that infected tons and tons of systems

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:55.875
because everyone was using this routine,

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:59.212
which was a common trusted routine from trusted sources,

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:03.115
and yet it had a vulnerability in it
that an attacker could exploit.

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:06.252
And those vulnerabilities were discovered. Guess what?

00:08:06.686 --> 00:08:11.123
Over here in this phase, right
when it was the most expensive, after it had been released.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:14.527
So, again, we want to try to eliminate that

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:17.463
or at least find it much earlier in the process, if we can.

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:20.399
And using trusted sources, at least, helps.

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:22.602
It doesn't eliminate the risk, but it helps.

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.804
Standard architectures.

00:08:24.804 --> 00:08:27.807
I want to have spelled out that when we're going

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:31.410
to do a certain approach,
this is how the system should look.

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:35.147
So we want to spell out in advance
what those would look like.

00:08:35.281 --> 00:08:37.083
There's a lot of different sources for that.

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:42.788
IBM has an Application Security
Architecture Reference website that you can take a look at.

00:08:42.788 --> 00:08:45.224
We'll reference here.

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:46.325
Also there are other sources,

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:50.596
but you want to be able to spell this out
for your whole organization so that they can see that.

00:08:51.264 --> 00:08:52.532
Mistakes to avoid.

00:08:52.532 --> 00:08:54.667
Again, we're going to go back to our friends at OWASP.

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:57.637
They have a thing called the OWASP Top Ten list.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:03.242
And what's really interesting in this is they're showing
what are the top ten vulnerabilities that we keep seeing.

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:05.511
And it's very interesting to look at this over time.

00:09:05.978 --> 00:09:08.514
Look, back in 2017, what were the top ten?

00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:12.118
And look now and the more recent one, 2021?

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:16.656
And what you'll find is there have been
a little bit of shifting here, but unfortunately,

00:09:16.656 --> 00:09:20.493
it's sort of the same list,
which means we're not getting better.

00:09:20.493 --> 00:09:23.229
We're not making these defects extinct.

00:09:23.496 --> 00:09:26.332
We're just kind of shuffling the order a little bit.

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:30.102
But things like buffer overflows and other types of

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:33.573
input validation errors have been around for a long time.

00:09:33.773 --> 00:09:36.242
But this is a great learning resource.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:38.511
So again, I refer you to OWASP.

00:09:38.511 --> 00:09:41.814
And then finally,
this notion is really starting to build some momentum.

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:44.183
The idea of a software bill of materials.

00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:49.522
That is, I want to know where everything came from,
kind of a supply chain, if you think of it this way.

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:52.258
I want to know all the components that I'm using.

00:09:52.592 --> 00:09:57.296
I want to know where they came
from, their origins, the libraries that they came from,

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:59.298
and where those are sourced.

00:09:59.298 --> 00:10:04.837
I want to know what dependencies exist amongst these
different components and have those really spelled out.

00:10:05.071 --> 00:10:07.173
I don't want any surprises in that.

00:10:07.173 --> 00:10:11.577
I want to know the versions of all of these
that I've used across all of my different systems.

00:10:11.811 --> 00:10:17.049
And if I'm building applications, I need to know
which versions are used because those will change over

00:10:17.049 --> 00:10:22.054
time, need to be updated, and I need to be able to know
what vulnerabilities might exist in those.

00:10:22.221 --> 00:10:25.157
Again, refer to Log4J.

00:10:25.358 --> 00:10:29.095
Go and do a web search on that
if you want to find out more about that example.

00:10:29.362 --> 00:10:32.498
That's a good example where if we have a software

00:10:32.498 --> 00:10:35.635
bill of materials
and we get hit by one of those vulnerabilities,

00:10:35.935 --> 00:10:39.739
then we have a much better idea of all the different places
that we need to make changes

00:10:39.972 --> 00:10:44.644
and we can recover much more quickly
and hopefully eliminate a certain amount of this cost.

00:10:45.745 --> 00:10:46.312
Okay.

00:10:46.312 --> 00:10:50.483
Now, we've covered the software development
lifecycle, secure coding,

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:53.753
and we're now going to take a look at vulnerability testing.

00:10:53.986 --> 00:10:56.822
We'd like to test for security throughout the process.

00:10:56.822 --> 00:11:02.128
Remember, one of the things I mentioned in the DevSecOps
process is to use more automation,

00:11:02.128 --> 00:11:03.663
that is, use more tooling.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:09.268
And two major classes of those tools are static application
security testing, SAST.

00:11:09.468 --> 00:11:12.605
And DAST: dynamic application security testing.

00:11:12.605 --> 00:11:15.908
You figured if a one was going to be static,
the other was going to be dynamic, right?

00:11:16.308 --> 00:11:18.210
Okay, what's the difference in those two?

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:22.548
Well,
the static is often referred to as “white box” testing.

00:11:22.782 --> 00:11:27.687
It's called that
because we can look inside and feed it the source code.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:30.122
So it's like it's looking inside our code.

00:11:30.256 --> 00:11:32.591
So the box is open in that sense.

00:11:33.092 --> 00:11:36.696
The black box dynamic, instead of giving it

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:40.766
source code, we're actually obscuring
what the source code is.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.035
It's going to look at an executable system.

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:44.870
Think about it differently.

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:49.709
In this case, we're going to do
maybe after the coding phase, I can run one of these tools,

00:11:49.709 --> 00:11:53.979
I can feed my source code into it,
and it will look for vulnerabilities

00:11:53.979 --> 00:11:56.682
and tell me about those, the ones that it can identify.

00:11:56.982 --> 00:12:01.353
And the great thing about that is we're moving
this much earlier.

00:12:01.454 --> 00:12:05.257
We're doing a shift left in the dev process
and finding those bugs

00:12:05.458 --> 00:12:09.361
at the earliest stage
that they were introduced, therefore, reducing cost.

00:12:10.196 --> 00:12:12.498
Even if I can't do it in that phase, I’d

00:12:12.498 --> 00:12:15.534
at least like to catch it during one of these test phases.

00:12:15.534 --> 00:12:19.638
So it's a little bit later in the development cycle,
but it's still before

00:12:19.638 --> 00:12:22.742
we hit release when everything gets super expensive.

00:12:23.042 --> 00:12:25.644
So what I'd like to do is run both of these.

00:12:25.678 --> 00:12:28.114
In other words, it's not an either/or.

00:12:28.447 --> 00:12:31.417
It's really both/and.

00:12:31.417 --> 00:12:34.887
Because there's advantages to both of these,

00:12:35.321 --> 00:12:39.959
the source code
scanner will find certain types of vulnerabilities.

00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:43.162
The dynamic scanner will find other types.

00:12:43.195 --> 00:12:47.199
So you really need to use them both together
and you use them throughout this process.

00:12:47.199 --> 00:12:52.271
And again, think about that cyclical devSecOps process
and you can see how this would fit in

00:12:52.404 --> 00:12:56.308
and how the tools
now become the enabler to do better security.

00:12:57.076 --> 00:13:01.981
One other sort of tool that's being used in this space
a lot for application development.

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:05.017
It wasn't initially thought of in that context
by a lot of people,

00:13:05.017 --> 00:13:08.020
but it's really becoming popular and that is chatbots.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:14.593
These large language models
with generative capabilities can also generate code.

00:13:14.593 --> 00:13:19.298
So a chatbot is good at generating code very quickly.

00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:23.435
You could say “write me a routine” in, say, Python

00:13:23.435 --> 00:13:27.473
that will find all the ways to make change for a dollar
and it'll spit it right out just like that.

00:13:27.773 --> 00:13:31.443
So that's a nice feature that you can have
the chatbot write code for you.

00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:34.446
It can also debug code.

00:13:34.613 --> 00:13:37.683
So if I've got stuck at some point

00:13:37.683 --> 00:13:42.254
during the development of some routine,
I could send it into a chatbot

00:13:42.254 --> 00:13:47.026
and maybe it will find what my vulnerability is
or what the bug is that I'm trying to deal with.

00:13:47.593 --> 00:13:49.228
So that's all good stuff.

00:13:49.228 --> 00:13:51.330
However, there's a downside.

00:13:51.831 --> 00:13:56.535
The downside is
it could also potentially inject vulnerabilities.

00:13:57.069 --> 00:14:01.273
So that is, when we get source code back,
we could inspect that.

00:14:01.273 --> 00:14:05.444
But probably you went to the chatbot
because you had a lot of code

00:14:05.444 --> 00:14:09.515
you wanted it to write for you, not just 12 lines of code,
because that you could have done on your own.

00:14:09.915 --> 00:14:13.319
So are you going to inspect all of that
and make sure that the chatbot either

00:14:13.319 --> 00:14:16.455
didn't make a mistake that introduces a vulnerability, or

00:14:16.622 --> 00:14:20.893
maybe it's been engineered in a way or someone has hacked it

00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:25.030
and it's intentionally putting a backdoor in
so that all of your data

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:28.801
is shared out with someone else,
or that malware is introduced without your knowledge.

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:30.402
So that's a big risk.

00:14:30.402 --> 00:14:34.006
It's not necessarily a trusted source,
like an open source library

00:14:34.006 --> 00:14:37.643
where we've had potentially thousands of eyes looking at it
and inspecting it.

00:14:37.776 --> 00:14:41.981
In this case, it just came right straight from the source,
and now you're putting it right into your code.

00:14:41.981 --> 00:14:43.482
So there's a big risk there.

00:14:43.482 --> 00:14:46.919
And another thing is, if I'm using it to do debugging,

00:14:46.919 --> 00:14:49.622
then that means I'm feeding my source code into this system.

00:14:50.055 --> 00:14:53.392
And this system might expose intellectual property.

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:55.895
If this code I have is considered

00:14:56.662 --> 00:15:01.066
confidential, if it's a trade secret,
if it's something that we might copyright.

00:15:01.066 --> 00:15:04.737
Now I've just taken that and I've given it directly to

00:15:04.904 --> 00:15:09.675
what is essentially to the Internet, potentially,
because it can now take that information and learn on it.

00:15:09.675 --> 00:15:12.378
It might use it might re-use your code in some other case.

00:15:12.678 --> 00:15:15.948
There was one major company
that, in fact, had that happen to them

00:15:16.081 --> 00:15:19.151
where their developers were using a chatbot to do debugging.

00:15:19.451 --> 00:15:24.690
And it turned out they were releasing
their proprietary source code into the system.

00:15:24.924 --> 00:15:28.227
And the company decided
that's not really what we want to be doing.

00:15:28.327 --> 00:15:30.162
So we need to stop that.

00:15:30.138 --> 00:15:33.966
OK, so that's a quick look at application security.

00:15:34.066 --> 00:15:38.137
What we've tried to cover are ways to introduce process,

00:15:38.971 --> 00:15:41.073
do a more continuous process,

00:15:41.373 --> 00:15:47.379
and use tooling in order to improve the security
of our systems and leverage the best of the learning

00:15:47.379 --> 00:15:52.184
from sources like OWASP so that we don't keep making
the same mistakes over and over again.

00:15:52.518 --> 00:15:56.255
It feels a lot if you've been doing security
like the movie Groundhog Day,

00:15:56.255 --> 00:15:59.858
we just keep reliving the same problems
over and over and over again.

00:16:00.125 --> 00:16:04.063
So let's learn from those
and use those good resources to not keep doing that.

00:16:04.863 --> 00:16:07.199
So now we've covered application security.

00:16:07.499 --> 00:16:12.471
In our next video, we're going to move over
to cover data security, which is the crown jewels.

00:16:12.471 --> 00:16:16.208
That's the thing
that we ultimately need to protect in most of these cases.

00:16:16.208 --> 00:16:21.280
And so that you don't miss that,
make sure that you hit it like, subscribe, and notify.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:31.323
And that way you'll catch the next video when it comes out.

